When you apply concrete types to a generic declaration, you get a concrete instance.

### A concrete generic struct

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Box type A = |
        value A,
    |

    int_box Box of I32 = Box(42)
    string_box Box of String = Box("hello")
]

`Box of I32` and `Box of String` are different types. You cannot assign one to the other.

### A concrete generic choice

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Maybe type A ::
        Some | value A |,
        Empty,
    ;

    name Maybe of String = Maybe::Some("Priya")
]

`Maybe of String` stores a `String` payload. `Maybe of I32` stores an `I32` payload.

Generic shapes stay fixed while eligible numeric origins in their known fields
can resolve within one body. This doesn't infer an unknown shape or a
nonnumeric type argument. See @#generic-inference (Generic inference).
